The first test in my journey of x86 assembly is basic addition, this will be done through the addition.asm file, its a simple program that uses ax and bx, the program stores the immediate value of 5 in ax, 8 in bx, then adds ax and bx. the expeceted result is 000Dh.

Using the N, or single step command on the monitor, execute it at 100, assuming CS is set to 0000 and that you already loaded the program at 0000:0100 using the laoder, you get this output:

SW0000:0100, 05B8- 05B8,
0102 BB00- BB00,                                                                                             
0104 0008- 0008,                                                                                             
0106 D801- D801,                                                                                             
0108 FEEB- FEEB,                                                                                             
010A FFFF-                                                                                                   
.N 0000- 0D 100,                                                                                             
 0103- BB .#                                                                                                 
.XAX=0005-                                                                                                   
.N 0103- BB ,                                                                                                
 0106- 01                                                                                                    
.XBX=0008-                                                                                                   
.N 0106- 01 ,                                                                                                
 0108- EB                                                                                                    
.XAX=000D-

Notice the single step into 100, then it outputs the byte BB, we use <CR> to exit the single step and inspect the AX reg with the X command on the monitor. It is now populated with 0005h, we step again and inspect the BX monitor, it's populated with 0008h. Finally, we step again to add, this produces a result in AX of 000Dh, which is exactly what we wanted! So first trial of 8086 math is a success!

